===Adjective===
{{en-adj|honeyer|er}}

# [[involve|Involving]] or [[resemble|resembling]] [[#Noun|honey]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 5|1|2|text=So work the '''honey'''-bees, / Creatures that by a rule in nature teach / The act of order to a peopled kingdom.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Madison Cawein|chapter=One Day and Another: A Lyrical Eclogue|title=The Poems of Madison Cawein|volume=II (New World Idylls and Poems of Love)|location=Indianapolis, Ind.|publisher=[[w:Bobbs-Merrill Company|The Bobbs-Merrill Company]]|year=1907|page=8|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/poemscawein02cawerich/page/8/mode/1up|passage=Dim as the forming of / Dew in the warming of / Moonlight, they light on the petals; / All is revealed to them; / All!—from the sunniest / Tips to the '''honiest''' / Heart, whence they yield to them / Spice, through the darkness that settles.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=[The Children’s Corner] Wireless Telegraphy|journal=The Inglenook|volume=XIII|issue=1|location=Elgin, Ill.|publisher=[[w:Church of the Brethren|Brethren Publishing House]]|date=3 January 1911|page=19|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/inglenook191113126bret/page/19/mode/1up|column=1|passage=“I say,” it said, “don’t gran’ma make the hunkiest frosted cookies, though?” / “My, yes, an’ gran’pa’s bees the '''honiest''' honey?” flashed back from Station Mary.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Percy MacKaye|title=The Mystery Of Hamlet, King of Denmark, or What We Will: A Tetralogy by Percy MacKaye, in Prologue to The Tragicall Historie of Hamlet, Prince of Denmarke, by William Shakespeare|location=London|publisher=w:The Bodley Head|year=1950|year_published=1952|page=397|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.100069/page/n421/mode/1up|passage={{smallcaps|moll}} / Even to the least detail of love and duty / To win the dalliance of her majesty. / {{smallcaps|king hamlet}} / Dalliance? / {{smallcaps|moll}} / ’T is the '''honiest''' word that sticks / On my lord Osric’s tongue, like a dayfly’s wing / On a toad’s tongue-tip.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=[[w:Harper's Bazaar|Harper’s Bazaar]]|volume=95|year=1962|page=204|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Z9khAQAAMAAJ&q=honiest|passage=Its perfume is variously described as ‘divine’ and reminiscent of nuts. Of grapes it is the grapiest; of honey the '''honiest'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=P.T. Raju|title=Structural Depths of Indian Thought|location=Albany, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:SUNY Press|State University of New York Press]]|year=1985|pages=407–408|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=blFY9SIJlT0C&pg=PA408|isbn=9781438416786|passage=The ''Bṛhadāraṇyaka Upaniṣad'' speaks of the ''ātman'' as the honey (''madhu'') and as the “'''honiest'''” of all the honeys.}}
# Of a [[pale]] [[yellow]] to [[brownish]]-[[yellow]] [[colour]], like most types of [[#Noun|honey]].
# [[honey-sweet|Honey-sweet]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Stephen J[oseph] Mac Kenna|title=Handfast to Strangers: A Novel|volume=III|location=London|publisher=[[w:Chapman & Hall|Chapman and Hall]],{{nb...|193, Piccadilly}}|year=1876|page=137|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=y4iZ1GzpfiQC&pg=PA137|passage=But he answered the question with the '''honiest'''—Bohemian honey—of smiles: {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Émile Zola; unknown translator|chapter=II|title=[[w:L'Œuvre|His Masterpiece? (L’Œuvre.) Or, Claude Lantier’s Struggle for Fame. A Realistic Novel.]]|location=London|publisher=[[w:Henry Vizetelly|Vizetelly]] & Co.,{{nb...|42 Catherine Street, Strand}}|year=1886|page=54|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/dli.ministry.13970/page/54/mode/1up|passage=But he suddenly changed his mind and came back. “Just listen, Lantier,” he said, in the '''honeyest''' of tones, “I want a lobster painted.{{nb...}}”|origtext=fr:Mais il se ravisa et revint dire, de son air bonhomme&nbsp;: «&nbsp;Écoutez donc Lantier, j’ai besoin d’un homard…{{nb...}}&nbsp;»}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=[[w:Punch (magazine)|Punch, or The London Charivari]]|location=London|publisher={{...|Published at}} [T]he Office,{{nb...|10, Bouverie Street, E.C.4}}|date=9 November 1927|page=512|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sim_punch_july-06-december-28-1927_173/page/512/mode/1up|columns=2–3|passage=“Oh, my dears, I must tell you this. At dinner the other evening I made a really truly growly belted earl squirm. I had told him how much I liked a little speech he had made, when he turned on me in his most growly belted early manner. / “‘Do you say that, Lady Polly,’ he snorted, ‘because it is the truth or because it is the correct thing to say?’ / “‘Not being an earl,’ I replied in my '''honeyest''' voice, ‘I don’t recognise any difference between the two.’}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:William Lederer|William J[ulius] Lederer]]|title=Ensign O’Toole and Me|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:W. W. Norton & Company|W. W. Norton & Company, Inc.]]|year=1957|page=177|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/apdfEnsignOTooleAndMeReducedFileSize/page/n183/mode/1up|lccn=57-10638|passage=“We’ve placed your arms in that position, Miss Day,” I said in my '''honeyest''' voice, “because it’s the safest just in case you have a fracture.{{nb...}}”}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=Cal|volume=33–35|year=1970|page=1|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=6d0aAQAAMAAJ&q=honeyest|passage=Then this guy stalled at my desk, fishing for his wallet, giving me the '''honeyest''' smile.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Adrian Mitchell|title=Zoo of Dreams|location=London|publisher=[[w:Grolier#Orchard Books|Orchard Books]]|year=2001|page=37|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/zooofdreams0000mitc/page/n40/mode/1up|isbn=1 84121 817 0|passage=Then upside down by her tail she swings / And sings in the '''honeyest''' voice I’ve heard: / “One day I will grow a pair of wings / And become a furry Hummingbird.”}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Lee Siegel (professor and novelist)|Lee Siegel]]|title=Who Wrote the Book of Love?|location=Chicago, Ill.; London|publisher=[[w:University of Chicago Press|The University of Chicago Press]]|year=2005|page=17|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/whowrotebookoflo0000sieg/page/17/mode/1up|isbn=0-226-75700-5|passage=It was Neezer who had found Sally for us at his church, where, he testified, the woman sang “with the '''honeyest''' voice you ever heard. If her singing don’t make the Lord happy, nothing does.”}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Dave Segal|title=The Zombies: Odessey and Oracle 50th Anniversary|journal=[[w:The Stranger (newspaper)|The Stranger]]|volume=26|issue=34|location=Seattle, Wash.|date=19 April 2017|page=37|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/thestranger_26_34/page/n36/mode/1up|column=3|passage=After hundreds of listens, ''{{w|Odessey and Oracle}}'' still sounds like a paragon of poignant psychedelic pop, rendered in orchestral splendor and adorned with indelible melodies that are to cry for, sung in the '''honeyest''' of tones by the angelically melancholy [[w:Colin Blunstone|[Colin] Blunstone]].}}

